# NOAA Precipitation Data {#sec-noaa-data} 



In rainingtables, we're pulling data from the [NOAA Global Historical Climatology Network Daily (GHCNd)](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/land-based-station/global-historical-climatology-network-daily). Let's take a look and review NOAA's notes on these data to understand what we're working with.

As [NOAA explains](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/land-based-station/global-historical-climatology-network-daily), the GHCNd is an integrated database of daily climate summaries from more than 100,000 weather stations across 180 countries and territories worldwide. In addition to data on total precipitation, summaries provide minimum and maximum temperature, snowfall, and snow depth. 

Weather stations vary in (a) how many fields and observations they provide and (b) how long they've been operational:    

(a) Not all weather stations report all data fields. About half of stations only report precipitation.  
(b) Stations don't necessarily start reporting data on the same day. Some stations have reported data for less than a year, some more than 175 years. Some previously reported data but have since ceased operating.   

We can see this in action:



## Mind Your Metadata

With clean, accessible, and detailed data like NOAA's, you may be tempted to jump into analysis and overlook key metadata and data exploration. **Resist!** Some metadata fields contain user notes from our nice NOAA nerds. They want you to know what you're looking at. 

🌂 

As an example, let's pick iconic places in [a few rainy cities](https://qz.com/rainiest-wettest-driest-us-cities#the-wettest-cities-in-america--and-the-driest) to pull data from: 

| Rainy City | Iconic Place | Zip Code |
| ---------- | ------------ | -------- |
| Syracuse, NY | Erie Canal Museum | 13202 | 
| Portland, OR | Rose Garden in Washington Park | 97205 |
| Grand Rapids, MI | Grand Rapids Public Museum | 49504 |

Let's use their zipcodes to pull their precipitation data and walk through it.  


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# pull data for these zipcodes
zipcodes <- c("13202", "97205", "49504")

# not all zipcodes have a weather station in them
# safe_pull allows the function to continue if it returns a null result on one zipcode
safe_pull <- purrr::safely(pull_daily_rain)

# pulling data from the past three months
rain_list <- purrr::map(zipcodes, function(z) {
  safe_pull(
    api_key = Sys.getenv("NOAA_CDO_KEY"),
    zipcode = z,
    date_start = Sys.Date() - months(3),
    date_end = Sys.Date()
  )
}) |> purrr::set_names(zipcodes)

example_table <- rain_list |>
    purrr::map("result") |>
    purrr::compact() |>
    purrr::imap_dfr(function(df, zip) {
        df |>
            mutate(zipcode = zip)
    }) |>
    bind_rows()

```
:::


Here's how our data are formatted when coming from NOAA's API (first ten rows):


