See Column 4, `attributes`? These are data notes from NOAA. They help us understand what we're seeing if we can crack the code. How can we interpret `attributes`? Look at the `attributes` values in zipcode 49504 observations. How do we find out what "N" means?  

The answer: it takes a bit of fishing on NOAA's website. 🎣

🐟 Fish 1: metadata manuals. 

Most public data sources have at least one metadata manual available to help you interpret their data. NOAA's no exception:  

- When we look at NOAA's Climate Data Online [Dataset Discovery page](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/cdo-web/datasets), we see a link to NOAA's [GHCN Documentation](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/pub/data/cdo/documentation/GHCND_documentation.pdf).  
- Scroll down to page 3, and under `Table 2 (Quality Flag/Attribute)`, we see: "N = failed naught check". 

Okay, what is a naught check? 🧐 

🐠 Fish 2: more metadata mining.

- With a bit more fishing, we find a second webpage, [Global Historical Climatology Network - Daily (GHCN - Daily), Version 3](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/metadata/geoportal/rest/metadata/item/gov.noaa.ncdc%3AC00861/html#). On this page, we spot a [GHCNd README File](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/data/global-historical-climatology-network-daily/doc/readme.txt), open it, and scroll down. Again we see "N = failed naught check" under QFLAG1, "the quality flag for the first day of the month." 

🐡 Fish 3: ask the experts! 🙋‍♀️


☂️

SELF NOTES:
* RE: Grand Rapids, this is from two weather stations, and the metadata are telling you it failed checks ("N").
* Have to look up via the [Climate Data Online Search](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/cdo-web/search?datasetid=GHCND) to make sure you're using current stations with good coverage. Like here: https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/cdo-web/datasets/GHCND/locations/CITY:US260009/detail#stationlist. Only three weather stateions are at 100% and are through current day. 

Let's align this with what we see in NOAA's [GHCN Documentation](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/pub/data/cdo/documentation/GHCND_documentation.pdf),
which you can find reviewing their Climate Data Online [Dataset Discovery page](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/cdo-web/datasets).

Their notes on daily summaries, with additional documentation [here](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/pub/data/cdo/documentation/GHCND_documentation.pdf):

:::{.callout-note}
### Daily Summaries

Global Historical Climate Network includes daily land surface observations from around the world. The GHCNd was developed to meet the needs of climate analysis and monitoring studies that require data at a sub-monthly time resolution (e.g., assessments of the frequency of heavy rainfall, heat wave duration, etc.). The dataset includes observations from World Meteorological Organization, Cooperative, and CoCoRaHS networks. If observed, the station dataset includes max and minimum temperatures, total precipitation, snowfall, and depth of snow on ground. Some U.S. station data are typically delayed only 24 hours.

:::

